meta {
  name: Update a book
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

patch {
  url: {{host}}/books/{{bookId}}
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "title": "Refactoring, second edition",
    "totalCopies": 3
  }
}

docs {
  200. PATCH semantics: the fields not sent here - isbn and author - are left alone rather
  than blanked. A request that sends nothing at all is a 400, because that is almost always
  a client serializing the wrong object and answering 200 would hide it.

  totalCopies moves the available count by the same delta, atomically, so a copy that is out
  on loan stays out. Lowering it below the number of copies currently lent is a 422: the
  request is well-formed and collides with no other record, but a business rule refuses the
  value itself.

  The two kinds of change are guarded differently on purpose. Title, author and ISBN go
  through the optimistic concurrency stamp, so two librarians renaming the same book
  concurrently produces a 409 for the second. The copy counts never do - a librarian adding
  copies while a reader borrows one is not a conflict, and both changes must apply.
}
